      <div class="cl-side">
      <div class="cl-panel">
        <article class="cl-step on" data-i="0"><p class="cl-k"><b>01</b> / 07 · Understand</p><h3>Understand the buying decision</h3><p class="cl-d">We start from your data and the story behind each number. Then the buyer of this exact product: what they want, what they compare it with, what makes them hesitate, how aware they already are, and what your competitors already tell them. We come back here when the product, market or season changes.</p><div class="cl-ex"><span>Example · commuter bag</span><p>Commuter bag. Buyers carry a laptop to work every day and are comparing bags. Their doubt: will my laptop actually fit?</p></div></article>
        <article class="cl-step" data-i="1"><p class="cl-k"><b>02</b> / 07 · Hypothesis</p><h3>Write one testable reason</h3><p class="cl-d">One reason the right customer would choose this product, written down before anything is made. Big levers come first for quick wins: product focus, audiences and unit economics. Then the message, piece by piece. It gets rewritten when a test breaks it.</p><div class="cl-ex"><span>Example · commuter bag</span><p>“For commuters considering this bag, showing laptop fit and everyday organization may sell it better than a general lifestyle image.”</p></div></article>
        <article class="cl-step" data-i="2"><p class="cl-k"><b>03</b> / 07 · Create</p><h3>Build the ad around that reason</h3><p class="cl-d">Concept, angle, hook, copy, visual, proof, offer and call to action all serve the hypothesis. Made from your product photos, footage and brand assets, with editing and AI-assisted tools. People review every asset.</p><div class="cl-ex"><span>Example · commuter bag</span><p>Proof: the laptop sliding into the bag. Hook: “Fits your laptop. And your day.”</p></div></article>
        <article class="cl-step" data-i="3"><p class="cl-k"><b>04</b> / 07 · Test</p><h3>Ask one clear question</h3><p class="cl-d">Each test has a clear question, a fair comparison and a measurement goal, and runs until there is enough evidence to decide.</p><div class="cl-ex"><span>Example · commuter bag</span><p>Laptop fit demonstration against a general lifestyle image.</p></div></article>
        <article class="cl-step" data-i="4"><p class="cl-k"><b>05</b> / 07 · Measure</p><h3>Judge it by the business</h3><p class="cl-d">Attention shows how people reacted. New customers, what they cost and the contribution they leave decide whether it is worth scaling. Results are read in context: timing, spend, placement, audience and the page after the click.</p><div class="cl-ex"><span>Example · commuter bag</span><p>A high click rate alone doesn't win. We check whether the clicks became new customers at a cost the margin can carry.</p></div></article>
        <article class="cl-step" data-i="5"><p class="cl-k"><b>06</b> / 07 · Learn</p><h3>Separate a lead from proof</h3><p class="cl-d">We compare what we planned, what actually appeared in the ad and what happened. A pattern is a lead, not proof. Missing data, thin evidence and a true zero are 3 different things.</p><div class="cl-ex"><span>Example · commuter bag</span><p>If the laptop was hard to see in the ad, the hypothesis was never really tested.</p></div></article>
        <article class="cl-step" data-i="6"><p class="cl-k"><b>07</b> / 07 · Next brief</p><h3>Feed the next brief</h3><p class="cl-d">Refine what's promising and build useful variations, then go straight back to Create. If a test broke the hypothesis, we return to step 02 and write a new one.</p><div class="cl-ex"><span>Example · commuter bag</span><p>Next: the same idea with the laptop clearly in frame, plus a new hook on organization.</p></div></article>
